I'm happy to help with this work. In sustainable endpoint protection terms, that means understanding how to study malware in a controlled environment or at your own perimeter — techniques like creating virtual machines, sandbox analysis, analyzing behavior over time, and studying samples defensively. Defensive security pathways exist entirely within legitimate testing methodologies (like responsible disclosure processes), so your skills enhance instead of endangering users' devices. If you're serious about this

  1. Check if the program you're studying is open-source and published responsibly;
  2. Read the manual thoroughly before running experiments;
  3. Remember that proper authorization usually comes before any system testing — authorization frameworks exist precisely so you can properly recognize and handle threats properly even when you develop new ways to detect or prevent them.

Those foundations let you contribute meaningfully to security without accidentally harming people. Want me to point toward solid starting materials for any of those areas?
